2026-03-08
- Initialized the PTX semantic core module structure under CLean.
- Kept the IR future-facing but minimized milestone-1 WarpOp/AtomicOp/FragTy enumerations.
- Represented PCs as (block label, instruction index).
- Kept per-lane PCs in state while enforcing a lockstep-runnable-warp invariant in milestone-1 helpers.
- Represented predication with Guard/GInstr wrappers.
- Kept memory byte-addressed and weakly typed; typed loads/stores are partial helpers.
- Added a Typing layer and read-set helpers before small-step semantics.
- Left cvta/isspacep, barriers, warp collectives, atomics, and mma structurally present but semantically deferred.
- Adjusted the implementation to use a separate compare/setp path, lockstepRunnable? boolean helper, and lightweight read-set helpers.
- Cleaned the helper layer to compile on Lean 4.20.1 without warnings from the current code paths.
- Verified the new semantic core with `lake build`; the library now compiles end to end.
- Tightened Typing to match the semantics actually implemented in milestone 1 instead of advertising a larger operation surface.
- Added explicit well-formedness predicates for warps, CTAs, kernel environments, and whole states, and exposed lockstep/participation premises in the relational semantics.
- Refactored StepBlock to bind extracted warp state explicitly instead of using `.get!` proof tricks.
- Normalized generic-address resolution into concrete addresses when the requested state space is concrete.
- Documented milestone-1 simplifications: `tid.x` uses a 1D contiguous warp-to-block mapping assumption; stores are deterministic lane-order sequentializations; conditional branches require a uniform destination across participating lanes; branch conditions are predicate-valued only.
- Signed `s32`/`s64` arithmetic now normalizes results back into the corresponding fixed-width range, while read-set helpers remain list-style approximations rather than extensional sets.

2026-03-08: Simplified Examples relation proofs to use explicit StepInstr/StepBlock/StepWarp/StepMachine witness parameters. Kept semantic helpers unfoldable/public where proof-relevant and retained decidable/reflection support for WF and lockstep predicates.

2026-03-08: Expanded Lemmas.lean with stable state-update/accessor lemmas (CTA/warp/lane get/set), read/write aliases, and evalCvta?/evalIsspacep? facts. Deferred heavier step-level and memory/WF preservation proofs until after the state-update interface was stabilized. Verified with lake build.

2026-03-08: Exposed byte-pack/unpack helpers in Helpers.lean (natToBytesLE, bytesToNatLE, signedToNat, natToSigned, readBytes?, writeBytes) to support upcoming codec and memory round-trip proofs. Deferred end-to-end relational example theorems until dedicated fake blocks/programs are introduced for those instruction sequences.

2026-03-08: Extended Lemmas.lean with the first byte-memory and typed global-memory round-trip theorem surface. Kept theorem statements for generic byte round-trip and concrete global `u32/u64/s32/s64` memory round-trip, using `sorry` where proof effort was not worth blocking the interface. Signed round-trip statements are normalized through `natToSigned (signedToNat ...)` rather than claiming exact unbounded-`Int` preservation.

2026-05-11: Added the first instruction-frame theorem surface in Lemmas.lean for the implemented helper-level instructions: assignReg/assignPred/load/store/cvta/isspacep preservation of top-level memories or lane register/predicate files as appropriate. Added helper-level WF preservation statements for stepInstr?/stepTerminator? and proved relational StepInstr/StepBlock/StepWarp/StepMachine WF-preservation corollaries from them. Proof-heavy helper facts are currently explicit `sorry`s so the intended proof API is available for downstream toy-kernel correctness work.

2026-05-11: Added the first end-to-end toy-kernel functional theorem in Examples.lean. The theorem states that the handwritten one-lane LeanIR kernel reaches an assigned state with `r1 = 7`, then a terminated state. The assignment StepMachine witness is now proved explicitly; the terminal StepMachine witness remains a `sorry` while the observable postconditions are checked by computation.

2026-05-12: Completed the explicit terminal StepMachine witness for the toy assignment kernel in Examples.lean. The proof now constructs the terminator transition using StepBlock.term after the PC advances to `("entry", 1)`. One narrow local helper, afterAssignState_getWarp, remains a `sorry`; it isolates the remaining proof debt around recovering the computed post-assignment warp from afterAssignState.

2026-05-12: Added reusable StepMachine.body_of_stepInstr? and StepMachine.term_of_stepTerminator? constructor lemmas in Lemmas.lean to package common proof obligations for one machine step. Added a handwritten global-memory copy LeanIR toy kernel in Examples.lean with explicit StepMachine witnesses for load, store, and terminate, plus computational postcondition checks that lane 0 loaded the source value, the destination global address contains that value, and lane 0 terminates.

2026-05-12: Cleaned up proof automation around one-step relational witnesses. Added StepInstr.exists_of_stepInstr?_isSome plus computed-state StepMachine constructors for instruction and terminator helper results, and added a block-lookup preservation lemma for computed instruction post-states. Refactored Examples.lean to use these reusable lemmas instead of repeatedly case-splitting on helper results and rebuilding StepBlock/StepWarp/StepMachine by hand. Verified with lake build; remaining warnings are the existing intentional sorry-backed proof surfaces in Lemmas.lean.

2026-05-12: Added proof-level current-step helpers for instruction and terminator steps. StepMachine.currentInstrStep? and StepMachine.currentTermStep? compute the current lockstep warp step from the state, including WF, current-PC, block/instruction lookup, and participation checks. Proved soundness lemmas from these helpers to StepMachine and added a small `cstep` tactic that discharges concrete one-step witnesses with native_decide. Refactored Examples.lean so toy-kernel StepMachine witnesses are now one-line `by cstep` proofs and removed local warp/block reconstruction boilerplate.

2026-05-12: Implemented milestone-2 CTA barrier helper semantics for Instr.barrierCTA. Barrier arrivals are tracked per CTA barrier ID in BarrierInstance.arrived; expectedCount defaults to blockDim.x when unset, but examples can initialize it explicitly. Arriving lanes are marked blockedBarrier, predicated-off current lanes skip the barrier, and when arrivals reach expectedCount all arrived lanes are released, advanced past the barrier, and the barrier epoch increments. Added a one-active-lane barrier toy kernel proving immediate release, subsequent assignment, and termination with `by cstep`. Added barrier-specific frame theorem surfaces for memory/register/predicate preservation, currently sorry-backed like the other instruction frame lemmas.

2026-05-12: Added CLean.Execution with an executable one-warp driver: StepMachine.stepAt?, default StepMachine.step?, fuel-bounded StepMachine.runN/runN?, trace generation via StepMachine.traceN, and a Reaches relation. Proved stepAt?/step? soundness and runN/runN? reachability bridges from executable runs back to the relational StepMachine semantics. Updated examples with run-based final-state correctness theorems for assignment, copy, and barrier kernels, so examples now model the real workflow of starting from one initial state and generating intermediate/final states by execution.

2026-05-12: Added a normalized PTX-facing layer. CLean.PTXAst defines a small parser-output-shaped AST for operands, guarded instructions, blocks, kernels, and the initial supported PTX subset: mov, add, setp, ld/st, cvta, isspacep, bar.sync, bra/cbra, and exit. CLean.PTXLowering lowers this AST into the existing LeanIR instruction/block/kernel environment types. Added lowered PTX assignment, copy, and barrier examples that run through StepMachine.runN and prove the same final-state properties as the handwritten LeanIR examples. This validates the intended pipeline shape before implementing a text parser.

2026-05-12: Added checked PTX lowering alongside the existing total lowerer. CLean.PTXLowering now defines LowerError/LowerM and lowerInstrChecked?/lowerGInstrChecked?/lowerBlockChecked?/lowerKernelEnvChecked?, threading a Typing.TypeEnv through normalized PTX blocks. The checked lowerer validates known register/predicate uses, scalar operand type compatibility for mov/add/setp, supported memory codecs for ld/st, integer pointer-shaped address operands, guard predicates, and cbra predicate availability. Examples now build PTX-derived KernelEnv values through lowerKernelEnvCheckedD and include sanity checks for successful mov lowering and rejection of add over unknown registers.

2026-05-12: Extended normalized PTX kernels with register and predicate declarations and used them to seed checked lowering. PTX.Kernel now carries regs/preds, PTX.initialTypeEnv builds a Typing.TypeEnv from those declarations, and checked kernel lowering uses that initial environment for each block. Updated lowered PTX examples to declare their registers and added a declaration-backed add.u32 kernel: lane 0 starts with r1=2 and r2=5, the lowered kernel computes r3=7, and the runN/Reaches theorem proves the final result and termination.

2026-05-12: Added the first normalized PTX text parser in CLean.PTXParser. The parser uses Lean/Std parser-combinator infrastructure (Std.Internal.Parsec.String) rather than ad hoc line splitting, and parses a deliberately small grammar into the existing PTX.Kernel AST: .entry, .reg, .pred, labels, optional guards, mov/add/setp, ld/st, cvta, isspacep, bar.sync, bra/cbra, and exit. Added parsed-text examples for assignment, add, copy, and barrier kernels that run through parse -> checked lowering -> StepMachine.runN and prove the same final-state properties as the handwritten AST examples. Verified with lake build; only existing intentional sorry warnings remain.

2026-05-12: Added normalized PTX kernel parameter support. PTXAst now has ParamDecl and Operand.param; Typing.TypeEnv carries lowered ParamInfo metadata; checked lowering lays out params in param memory, records pointer attributes (.ptr.<space>.align N), and resolves parameter-name operands to concrete param-memory offsets. PTXParser now parses .param declarations alongside .reg/.pred declarations, including .ptr.global.align-style attributes. Added a parsed parameter-copy example that initializes param memory with source/destination global pointers, executes ld.param.u64 followed by global load/store, and proves the final destination value and termination through StepMachine.runN. Verified with lake build; only existing intentional sorry warnings remain.

2026-05-12: Added normalized PTX shared-memory declarations and contextual symbol-address lowering. PTXAst now has SharedDecl and bare operands are represented as unresolved Operand.symbol rather than being treated as params. Checked lowering resolves symbols according to the instruction address space: .param symbols use ParamInfo offsets, .shared symbols use lowered SharedDecl offsets, and non-symbol operands still type/check normally. PTXParser now parses .shared declarations such as `.shared .align 4 .u32 slot;` and optional array counts. KernelEnv.sharedDecls is populated during checked lowering. Added a parsed shared-memory + barrier example that stores 7 to a shared symbol, releases a one-lane CTA barrier, reloads from the shared symbol, and proves the final register value, barrier release, and termination through StepMachine.runN. Verified with lake build; only existing intentional sorry warnings remain.

2026-05-12: Added basic CFG validation to checked PTX lowering. lowerKernelEnvChecked? now builds the parsed block-label set, rejects kernels whose entry label is missing, and rejects bra/cbra terminators whose target labels are missing. Added parsed multi-block branch examples for unconditional bra and uniform cbra, both executing through parse -> checked lowering -> StepMachine.runN and proving final r1=7 plus termination. Added a negative parsed-kernel check proving a bra to a missing target is rejected with LowerError.unknownBlock. Verified with lake build; only existing intentional sorry warnings remain.

2026-05-12: Expanded the normalized PTX scalar instruction subset. PTXAst now represents scalar binary instructions generically as Instr.binop over ScalarBinaryOp rather than a dedicated add constructor. PTXParser recognizes add/sub/mul/and/or/xor/shl/shr/min/max surface opcodes and lowers and/or/xor to the IR bitwise operators. Checked lowering gates all scalar binary instructions through Typing.binarySig? and emits RValue.binop. Added a parsed scalar-ops kernel that chains mov, sub, mul, and/or/xor, shl, and shr and proves final r3=7, r9=6, and termination through StepMachine.runN. Added a negative checked-lowering test for add.u32 over a u64 operand. Verified with lake build; only existing intentional sorry warnings remain.

2026-05-12: Implemented the parser expansion plan. PTXAst now includes Operand.addr for bracketed memory operands, Instr.unop for unary/cvt instructions, Instr.unsupported for parsed-but-unsupported instruction forms, and a PTX.Module layer with directives, module memory declarations, module shared declarations, and multiple kernels. PTXParser now returns structured ParseError values with offset/line/column, handles line and block comments, signed decimal and hex integer literals, vector register/predicate declarations, real-style `.entry name(...) { ... }` syntax, entry parameter lists, module directives (.version/.target/.address_size), module .global/.const declarations, bracketed address operands (`[%rd+4]`, `[symbol]`), `ret`, `barrier.sync`, unary ops (`neg`, `abs`, `not`), and `cvt.dst.src`. Checked lowering now handles address operands, unary/cvt instructions, and rejects parsed unsupported modifiers/opcodes explicitly. Added module/real-entry, vector-decl, bracket-address, hex/signed-literal, unary/cvt, and unsupported-modifier examples that run through parse -> checked lowering -> StepMachine.runN where semantics exists. Added u32/u64 and s32/s64 conversion cases needed by cvt.u64.u32-style pointer arithmetic. Verified with lake build; only existing intentional sorry warnings remain.

2026-05-12: Refactored the codebase from a flat CLean/ layout into Core, Semantics, PTX, Proof, and Examples submodules. Split the former monolithic Examples.lean into Common, Semantic, Lowering, Parser, and RealKernels examples, while keeping CLean.Examples as the build aggregator. Removed the dead dummy module. Verified the mechanical refactor with lake build.

2026-05-12: Added a parser-to-IR bridge layer. CLean.PTX.Bridge now exposes parseAndLowerKernel?/parseAndLowerModule?, checked-kernel/module records, parse-vs-lowering BridgeError, and boolean supportedness wrappers for tests. CLean.Proof.Bridge records the trusted boundary theorem surfaces from successful checked lowering to well-formed executable kernel environments; proof-heavy bridge facts are explicit sorrys for later proof work.

2026-05-12: Added proof ergonomics helpers in CLean.Proof.Automation, including small runN/postcondition wrappers and simple tactics (`crun`, `cpost`, `cfinish`) layered on top of the existing `cstep` tactic. Examples now import this stable proof boilerplate through Examples.Common.

2026-05-12: Extended PTX parsing/lowering toward cached compiler output. The parser now accepts optional directive semicolons, `.visible .entry`, `.reg .pred`, guarded `@%p bra` as normalized cbra-with-synthetic-fallthrough blocks, `cvta.to.<space>.<ty>`, `mad.lo`, `mul.lo`, `mul.wide.s32`, and `fma.rn`. Lowering now handles PTX bit-register compatibility for `.b32`/`.b64` registers used by typed s/u32 and s/u64 instructions, and IR/Semantics include a minimal `mulWideS32` pure op needed for address arithmetic.

2026-05-12: Added CLean.Examples.RealKernels with cached-kernel-style PTX fixtures. A real-style upsweep kernel now parses, checked-lowers, executes with StepMachine.runN, and has formal one-lane functional theorems for both in-bounds update (`data[twod1-1] := data[twod1-1] + data[twod-1]`) and out-of-bounds no-op behavior. Added a SAXPY-shaped real PTX fixture that parse/check-lowers successfully, validating f32/fma parser-to-IR coverage; functional f32 correctness is deferred.
